Pair confirmed landed — ccip-router#3 and the #1826 amendment are exactly what we ratified: observation-identity, divergence as a first-class state, the named hook, and the deferral called out rather than buried. Congratulations Tiago — from live bug to landed spec amendment with load-bearing tests, with the design argued in public the whole way. Companion drafting starts now, citing those anchors directly.

@Tiago — two carry-overs from the message this crossed with. The mutation gate: formalize it as the recipe — with a lineage line (the house rule "prove the check can fail" plus the declared-vs-demonstrated distinction from the TSEI thread, automated) and the mutation set tied to the spec's normative claims, not just code: every MUST gets a mutant that must be KILLED, making the gate a conformance property of the specification itself. And credit where due — you killed two of your own three survivors before the proposal even circulated; first-valid-wins pinned by test is exactly the pin-or-declare our own rule required. That leaves exactly one known survivor: the detector inversion. One test asserting same-signer equivocation actually warns kills it — follow-up on #3 or first backlog item, your call, but let's not sit long on a suite where we know precisely which mutant survives.

@Jimmyshi — the observation/attestation separation is the companion's data-model spine, adopted structurally with your trichotomy: every profile declares what is being agreed — observation set, resolution verdict, or action — default A agreeing only on the observation set and resolving nothing. Front matter carries the three-state fault taxonomy your vocabulary surfaces, since classic BFT doesn't foreground it: honest vantage-conditioned divergence (explained by declared conditioning variables — not a fault), same-signer equivocation, and unexplained divergence (the Byzantine candidate). PBFT/HotStuff enter as exactly what you said — reference points profiles declare inheritance from, never imports.

And one honest note so it doesn't dissolve into the congratulations: your "deduplicate exact replay only, retain distinct signed messages" is stronger than the deferral we shipped — as amended, the base still discards same-value attestations by default, which is precisely the evidence consensus-class profiles need. You parked it as non-blocking and producer re-sync keeps it recoverable, so landing the pair was right — but I'm claiming it as the named follow-up base extension, not companion mist. It has a clean shape your framing exposes: key attestation identity on the signed message itself — the signature, NOT-NULL on every valid attestation — which sidesteps the NULL source_peer trap that forced the deferral. Observation identity stays (inputHash, namespace, value); attestation retention becomes a separate signature-keyed concern. The companion's quorum/consensus profiles will require that extension by name, cite the deferral note as anchor, and carry your envelope bindings (timestamp+payload now; instanceId, epoch, round, phase, payloadHash for consensus-class profiles).

@babyblueviper1 — execution_binding=external is the same separation one layer up: a signed judgment is not the claim that the approved action happened, just as preserved evidence is not agreement and resolution is not agreement. The companion carries all three refusals as one discipline — your §5 teeth, the base's divergence state, and the action boundary — and your verdict pipeline stays the reference consumer for the review pass.

Companion v0.1 draft is now the active work item on my side — against ccip-router#3, the #1826 clause, resolveDivergence, and the deferral anchor. It comes back to this thread for review when it's real.
